          "bodyHtml": "<p>When an individual is charged with DWI in North Carolina, two separate mechanisms are initiated. The first is the criminal court process in your county district court (such as Mecklenburg, Wake, or Guilford County). A DWI conviction results in statutory sentencing levels, fines, community service, and court-ordered license revocation.</p>\n<p>The second track is administrative and managed directly by the NCDMV. A civil license revocation (commonly 30 days) occurs immediately upon arrest if chemical testing shows a BAC of 0.08% or higher, or if a test is refused. In either case, regaining road access requires certified proof of insurance via Form DL-123 or an SR-22.</p>"
